WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.230
Reporter: Dropping a nuclear
bomb inside a hurricane.

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:05.740
Reporter: Drop a nuclear bomb on them.

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:07.937
Reporter: The president
suggested bombing a hurricane

00:00:07.937 --> 00:00:09.470
on multiple occasions.

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:11.837
Briefers reportedly told
the president, quote,

00:00:11.837 --> 00:00:13.010
"We'll look into that."

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:15.220
Narrator: Believe it or
not, President Donald Trump

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:18.970
isn't the first person to
suggest we nuke a hurricane.

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:22.010
Back in the '50s, a
meteorologist named Jack Reed

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:24.780
introduced the idea as a
way to prevent hurricanes

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:26.410
from reaching the coast.

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:28.338
Announcer: High- and low-pressure areas

00:00:28.338 --> 00:00:30.971
are recorded hourly at each station.

00:00:30.971 --> 00:00:32.443
Narrator: And people have been asking why

00:00:32.443 --> 00:00:35.890
we don't nuke hurricanes ever since.

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:39.440
In fact, the National Oceanic
and Atmospheric Administration

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.790
has been asked this question so much,

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:46.638
it has a web page dedicated
to answering just that.

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:51.770
Alex Wellerstein: So, if
you set off a nuclear weapon

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:54.180
in a hurricane, not only do
we still have a hurricane,

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:55.990
now you have a radioactive hurricane.

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:59.220
So, that's not a positive development.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.387
Narrator: For perspective,
a hurricane can generate

00:01:01.387 --> 00:01:03.770
the same amount of energy
throughout its lifetime

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:06.760
as 10,000 nuclear bombs.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:08.440
And the physical size of a hurricane

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:10.230
is a major issue as well.

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:14.370
Hurricane Irma measured 420 miles across.

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:18.870
That's 70 times as large as the
blast zone for "Little Boy,"

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:21.741
the nuclear bomb dropped over Hiroshima.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.750
So, detonating an atomic
bomb inside a hurricane

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:29.480
is sort of like poking
a rhino with a stick

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.980
and expecting it to just
get out of your way.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:33.820
It's not gonna happen.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:35.560
Oh, and then there's the issue of

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:38.470
highly radioactive nuclear fallout.

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:39.703
Wellerstein: Best-case scenario

00:01:39.703 --> 00:01:41.644
is that it rains out very quickly.

00:01:41.644 --> 00:01:44.560
And so the area that
you set the weapon off

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:46.800
becomes highly radioactive
and contaminated.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.850
That's your best-case scenario.

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:50.916
A more worse-case scenario is

00:01:50.916 --> 00:01:54.158
what if that somehow
stays in the hurricane

00:01:54.158 --> 00:01:57.025
and moves as the hurricane,
say, makes landfall?

00:01:57.025 --> 00:01:59.970
Narrator: In a worst-case
scenario, winds from the hurricane

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:02.410
would quickly distribute
the radiation throughout

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:04.300
the entire storm.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:08.283
Basically turning it from a
massive storm with wind and rain

00:02:08.283 --> 00:02:12.340
into a massive storm with
radioactive wind and rain

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:15.900
that's 10 times larger and
millions of times more powerful

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:19.060
than any nuclear bomb in history.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:22.715
Not to mention, hurricanes move fast.

00:02:22.715 --> 00:02:27.090
In 2012, Hurricane Sandy
traveled over 2,000 miles

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:30.130
from the Caribbean Sea
up the US East Coast

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:31.930
in just 10 days.

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:33.660
And we know from past experience

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:37.290
what nuclear fallout does to
an ocean and people nearby.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.670
Wellerstein: It was in 1954,
the United States set off

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:42.620
a very large thermonuclear
weapon in the Marshall Islands,

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:46.600
and it contaminated a
lot of fish in the area.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:49.580
Japan ended up having a
moratorium on fish consumption

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:51.890
for some time as a result of this.

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:54.429
And you could detect the
radioactivity in fish

00:02:54.429 --> 00:02:56.570
for thousands of miles.

00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:59.880
Narrator: Plus, scientists
estimated that 55%

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:03.740
of all cancers in those living
closest to the bomb site

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:07.170
might be attributed to the
bomb's nuclear fallout.

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:10.230
So, if we had nuked, say, Hurricane Sandy,

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:13.590
not only would there
have been the $65 billion

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:15.010
in damage repair,

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:17.110
but who knows how much
more it would have cost in

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:20.380
the additional damage to
fishing industries, healthcare,

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:23.300
and even the simplest things
you might not think about.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:24.720
Wellerstein: Because you
can't just rebuild the house.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.690
Now you've also got to remove, you know,

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:28.190
several inches of topsoil

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:30.420
and destroy the original material

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.590
and rebuild it from scratch
if you want people to be able

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:35.870
to live there without
increasing the cancer risk.

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:38.592
Narrator: Suffice it to
say, nuking a hurricane,

00:03:38.592 --> 00:03:42.970
as NOAA puts it, is "not a good idea."

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:45.148
Wellerstein: It's less of
a sort of doomsday scenario

00:03:45.148 --> 00:03:47.610
as it is just a, like, really bad idea.

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:49.223
No-benefit scenario.

